essay:
The Legion of Bouz lost.

Let that marinate. The team we spent an entire column venerating as the league's lone undefeated — the inaugural champion that had the preseason doubters issuing public apologies in the group chat — got ambushed by the Sleeping Giants 147.3 to 143.72. Kyren Williams' 27.1 and Stefon Diggs' 19.6 outdueled Mahomes' 27.72 and McCaffrey's 23.9 in a game that flipped the top of the standings into a five-alarm pileup: Kittler, Legion, Sleeping Giants, Rasheeing, and Ken-obi all sit at 4-1.

Five teams. Same record. One parking spot at the top. The preseason blueprint — Ken-obi first, Noble second, MHJTIME third — is now a relic filed somewhere between Blockbuster membership cards and confident Week 1 takes.

{{media:week5-essay-opener}}

Meanwhile, MHJTIME remembered it had Ja'Marr Chase on the roster. Chase torched Burden for 29.0 points, Rico Dowdle — a name that wasn't in anybody's preseason projections — exploded for 31.9, and the "what if" franchise dropped 177.94 in the week's highest-scoring performance. That's the same team that was 1-3 entering the week. The same team whose WR trio we diagnosed as a "crime scene" seven days ago. The preseason said "toilet-bowl runner-up in 2023, semifinalist in 2024, top three in 2025." After four weeks, we amended that to a eulogy. After five weeks, the patient is sitting up and asking for its chart back.

{{media:week5-mhjtime-explosion}}

But the week's best story happened at the bottom of the standings. Chudders Football Team won a game. An actual, regulation, not-a-typo football game. Emeka Egbuka's 27.8 and Jacory Croskey-Merritt's 26.0 powered a 139.34-point effort that edged Ghastly Grayskull Gang by 2.02 — a margin so thin it makes last week's 1.64-point Ken-obi loss look like a blowout. The all-time series between these two is now tied at four, and the team that was "playing for 2026" just stole a 2025 souvenir.

{{media:week5-chudders-first-win}}

The Lock of the Week — Legion minus three over the Sleeping Giants — crashed and burned for the second consecutive week. Two straight Locks. Two straight losses. The column's gambling credibility is approaching Father Time's Elo: cratering fast with no floor in sight. Week 4 picks went 3-3, dragging the cumulative record to 14-10. Still profitable. Still embarrassing when it matters most.

At the season's midway approach — five weeks through fourteen — the Jailyard has settled into a shape nobody predicted and everyone should fear. Five teams sharing the penthouse. Two teams at 0-5 contemplating the basement. And the most explosive performance of the week coming from a 2-3 team that might be the most dangerous squad nobody's talking about. The standings say parity. The scoring says chaos. And the group chat? The group chat says check the Lock of the Week's track record before putting any faith in what comes next.
